        <div>
          <span class="badge badge-accent">EXECUTION ENGINE</span>
          <h2 class="fund-title">The Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU) Deep-Dive</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fund-lead">
        The <strong>Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)</strong> is the computational heart of every microprocessor core. It receives binary operands from processor registers and performs fundamental integer math (addition, subtraction), bitwise logic (AND, OR, XOR), shifts, and comparisons in a fraction of a nanosecond.
      </p>

      <!-- Top-Down Progression: ALU Architecture & Primitives -->
      <div class="concept-stream">
        <!-- 2.1 ADDITION PRIMITIVES -->
        <div class="concept-step-card">
          <div class="step-card-header">
            <span class="step-tag">2.1 ADDITION PRIMITIVE</span>
            <h3 class="step-card-title">The Full Adder &amp; Carry-Lookahead Chains</h3>
          </div>
          <p>
            Addition is the fundamental primitive from which almost all microprocessor arithmetic derives. While a <em>Half Adder</em> adds two bits, a <strong>Full Adder</strong> adds three bits (<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, and a Carry-In <em>C<sub>in</sub></em> from the preceding bit column):
          </p>
          <div class="formula-box">
            <div style="margin-bottom: 0.35rem;"><strong>Sum:</strong> <em>S</em> = <em>A</em> &oplus; <em>B</em> &oplus; <em>C<sub>in</sub></em></div>
            <div><strong>Carry-Out:</strong> <em>C<sub>out</sub></em> = (<em>A</em> &middot; <em>B</em>) + [<em>C<sub>in</sub></em> &middot; (<em>A</em> &oplus; <em>B</em>)] = <em>G</em> + (<em>P</em> &middot; <em>C<sub>in</sub></em>)</div>
          </div>
          <p class="small-text">
            Chaining 32 full adders together creates a <strong>32-bit Ripple-Carry Adder (RCA)</strong>. Because bit 31 must wait for the carry bit to ripple through all 31 preceding stages, propagation delay scales linearly: <em>O</em>(<em>N</em>). High-performance processors eliminate this ripple bottleneck using logarithmic <strong>Carry-Lookahead Adders (CLA)</strong> and parallel prefix trees (Kogge-Stone, Brent-Kung).
          </p>

          <!-- SCHEMATIC: ADDER ARCHITECTURES -->
          <div class="schematic-container">
            <div class="schematic-header">
              <span class="schematic-tag">DATAPATH SCHEMATIC</span>
              <span class="schematic-title">Ripple-Carry vs. Logarithmic Prefix (Kogge-Stone) Carry Network</span>
            </div>
            <div class="schematic-svg-wrap">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 540 230" class="schematic-svg" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                <!-- Top Half: Ripple Carry Chain -->
                <text x="25" y="24" font-weight="900" font-size="12" fill="#f38469">Ripple-Carry Adder: Linear Delay O(N)</text>
                <!-- 4 bit stages -->
                <rect x="25" y="35" width="85" height="42" rx="4" fill="#ffd8ce" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <text x="45" y="55" font-weight="800" font-size="10" fill="#f38469">FA Bit 0</text>
                <text x="35" y="68" font-size="8" fill="#242c33">Delay: 1 t_carry</text>

                <path d="M 110 56 L 140 56" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrowRed)"/>

                <rect x="145" y="35" width="85" height="42" rx="4" fill="#ffd8ce" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <text x="165" y="55" font-weight="800" font-size="10" fill="#f38469">FA Bit 1</text>
                <text x="155" y="68" font-size="8" fill="#242c33">Delay: 2 t_carry</text>

                <path d="M 230 56 L 260 56" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="2"/>

                <rect x="265" y="35" width="85" height="42" rx="4" fill="#ffd8ce" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <text x="285" y="55" font-weight="800" font-size="10" fill="#f38469">FA Bit 2</text>
                <text x="275" y="68" font-size="8" fill="#242c33">Delay: 3 t_carry</text>

                <path d="M 350 56 L 380 56" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="2"/>

                <rect x="385" y="35" width="115" height="42" rx="4" fill="#f38469" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="2"/>
                <text x="398" y="55" font-weight="900" font-size="10" fill="#f38469">FA Bit 31 (MSB)</text>
                <text x="395" y="68" font-size="8" fill="#242c33">Total: 32 Gate Delays!</text>

                <!-- Divider -->
                <line x1="20" y1="95" x2="520" y2="95" stroke="#cbd5e1" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>

                <!-- Bottom Half: Kogge Stone Parallel Tree -->
                <text x="25" y="118" font-weight="900" font-size="12" fill="#b7df69">Kogge-Stone Parallel Prefix Adder: Logarithmic Delay O(log2 N)</text>
                
                <!-- Inputs row -->
                <rect x="25" y="130" width="100" height="26" rx="3" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <text x="35" y="147" font-weight="800" font-size="9" fill="#b7df69">P0, G0 (Bit 0..7)</text>

                <rect x="155" y="130" width="100" height="26" rx="3" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <text x="162" y="147" font-weight="800" font-size="9" fill="#b7df69">P1, G1 (Bit 8..15)</text>

                <rect x="285" y="130" width="100" height="26" rx="3" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <text x="290" y="147" font-weight="800" font-size="9" fill="#b7df69">P2, G2 (Bit 16..23)</text>

                <rect x="415" y="130" width="100" height="26" rx="3" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <text x="420" y="147" font-weight="800" font-size="9" fill="#b7df69">P3, G3 (Bit 24..31)</text>

                <!-- Binary Tree Merging Nodes -->
                <circle cx="105" cy="180" r="12" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <text x="98" y="184" font-weight="900" font-size="9" fill="#151b20">&#8226;&#8226;</text>

                <circle cx="235" cy="180" r="12" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <text x="228" y="184" font-weight="900" font-size="9" fill="#151b20">&#8226;&#8226;</text>

                <circle cx="365" cy="180" r="12" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <text x="358" y="184" font-weight="900" font-size="9" fill="#151b20">&#8226;&#8226;</text>

                <circle cx="485" cy="180" r="12" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <text x="478" y="184" font-weight="900" font-size="9" fill="#151b20">&#8226;&#8226;</text>

                <!-- Tree Connections -->
                <line x1="75" y1="156" x2="105" y2="168" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <line x1="205" y1="156" x2="235" y2="168" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <line x1="335" y1="156" x2="365" y2="168" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
                <line x1="465" y1="156" x2="485" y2="168" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>

                <rect x="25" y="200" width="490" height="22" rx="3" fill="#b7df69"/>
                <text x="75" y="215" font-weight="900" font-size="10" fill="#ffffff">All 32 Carries Computed in Parallel: Only log2(32) = 5 Gate Delays!</text>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div class="schematic-caption">
              <strong>Figure 2.1:</strong> Delay comparison between Ripple-Carry and Parallel Prefix (Kogge-Stone) adders. While Ripple-Carry requires 32 sequential full adder delays for a 32-bit addition, a Kogge-Stone tree evaluates Generate (<em>G</em>) and Propagate (<em>P</em>) terms across logarithmic stages, reducing 32-bit carry delay from 32 steps down to only 5 steps.
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- THE PROBLEM & DESIGN TRADE-OFF: ADDER ARCHITECTURES -->
          <div class="tradeoff-box">
            <div class="tradeoff-badge">THE PROBLEM &amp; DESIGN TRADE-OFF</div>
            <div class="tradeoff-grid">
              <div class="tradeoff-col">
                <span class="to-label">&#9889; The Engineering Problem</span>
                <p>
                  Addition sits squarely on the single most critical timing path of the entire processor. Every branch comparison, load address computation, and loop counter depends on the adder finishing before the clock tick.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="tradeoff-col">
                <span class="to-label">&#9878; The Silicon Trade-off</span>
                <p>
                  <strong>Speed vs. Wire Density &amp; Area:</strong><br/>
                  &bull; <em>Ripple-Carry:</em> Tiny area (<em>O</em>(<em>N</em>)), low wiring congestion, but extremely slow (<em>O</em>(<em>N</em>) delay).<br/>
                  &bull; <em>Kogge-Stone:</em> Minimum possible delay (<em>O</em>(log <em>N</em>)), but explodes in silicon area and wiring tracks (<em>O</em>(<em>N</em> log <em>N</em>)), creating severe routing congestion in dense FinFET metallization layers.<br/>
                  &bull; <em>Brent-Kung:</em> Balanced compromise with bounded fan-out and intermediate area.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- INTERACTIVE ADDER DELAY & AREA EXPLORER -->
          <div class="sub-interactive-card" id="adderTradeoffWidget">
            <div class="sub-widget-header">
              <span class="sub-w-tag">INTERACTIVE SIMULATION</span>
              <h4 class="sub-w-title">Adder Architecture Delay vs. Area Trade-off Explorer</h4>
              <p class="sub-w-desc">
                Select an operand bit-width and adder topology to compare silicon area, critical path gate delays, and maximum achievable clock frequency:
              </p>
            </div>

